# Vulnerability details

## Impact
`getLoanStatus` assumes that if `positionId` is not a credit position ID, it must be a debt position ID. However, it doesn't properly handle the case where `positionId` is neither a valid debt position ID nor a valid credit position ID.

In the current implementation, if `positionId` is not a credit position ID, it directly accesses `state.data.debtPositions[positionId]` without checking if it is a valid debt position ID. If `positionId` is an invalid ID that doesn't exist in the `debtPositions` mapping, this will cause an exception and revert the transaction.

## Proof of Concept
Code link: [LoanLibrary.sol#L118-L140](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-size/blob/8850e25fb088898e9cf86f9be1c401ad155bea86/src/libraries/LoanLibrary.sol#L118-L140)

The function directly accesses `state.data.debtPositions[positionId]` without checking if `positionId` is a valid debt position ID.

A test case that demonstrates the bug
```solidity
// Test case: Accessing an invalid debt position ID
function testGetLoanStatusInvalidDebtPositionId() public {
    uint256 invalidPositionId = 999; // Assuming 999 is not a valid debt position ID
    vm.expectRevert(Errors.INVALID_POSITION_ID.selector);
    loanLibrary.getLoanStatus(state, invalidPositionId);
}
```
In this test case, we attempt to call `getLoanStatus` with an invalid debt position ID (assuming 999 is not a valid ID). We expect the function to revert with the `INVALID_POSITION_ID` error.

However, running the test will fail because the function directly accesses `state.data.debtPositions[invalidPositionId]`, causing an exception.

